From: Amirali Abdolrashidi <abdolrashidi@google.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:49:20 -0700
Subject: Vulkan: Restage deferred clear if invalidate drops

  For immediate-mode renderers (IMRs), framebuffer invalidation is
skipped for emulated formats due to the following feature flag to keep
content defined and save bandwidth:
* preferSkippingInvalidateForEmulatedFormats

This is performed in FramebufferVk::invalidateImpl(). Earlier in the
same function, deferred clears are dropped unconditionally. This means
that if the invalidation is skipped after a clear (which is dropped),
the framebuffer remains uninitialized, even if robust resource init is
enabled.

This change makes sure that the clear is applied if the invalidation is
skipped, e.g., due to the existence of emulated channels.

* Updated FramebufferVk::invalidateImpl() to only drop the pending
  deferred clears if the attachment content will be invalidated.

  * For depth/stencil, the check for dropDepthStencilClearOnInvalidate
    was moved below the preferToKeepContentsDefined evaluation.

 * Deferred clears are restaged before the robust resource init step.
    This guarantees that if an attachment is invalidated, robust init
    is applied prevent uninitialized memory leaks (e.g., during partial
    clears of packed D/S formats).

  * Sub-invalidation does not need to handle deferred clears because
    invalidateSub() already restages and flushes them prior to calling
    invalidateImpl().

* Added unit tests to RobustResourceInitTestES3 to make sure that the
  clears are applied if the invalidation is skipped.
  * InvalidateUndefinedThenReadBack
  * InvalidateUndefinedThenBlend
  * ClearThenRedirtyThenInvalidateThenReadBack
  * Similar tests for depth and stencil attachments.

Bug: chromium:568906910
Change-Id: If02f23fb18d99dd8ee3b06dc9bd2941a73b6350e
Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/angle/angle/+/8605819
